# Story: someone starts Arbor on a Mac as it comes, with macOS's own tiling on, and drags windows to the screen's edges as they always have.
# Arbor never fights macOS over a window: the left, right and top edges are macOS's (issue #3, rule 1; QUESTIONS Q23).
# The simulated display is 1440x900 with a 25-point menu bar.
open Safari
open Notes
open Mail
# Pushed against the left edge, Safari gets the left half of the screen from macOS. Arbor docks nothing and moves nothing.
drag 101 0 450
wait 1
expect floating 101
expect size 101 720x875
expect count docked 0
expect said macOS tiled this window; Arbor stood aside · ⌃⌥← docks it · Check Setup… in the menu
# Held in the menu bar, Notes fills the screen.
drag 102 700 0
wait 1
expect floating 102
expect size 102 1440x875
# Let go a few points short of the edge, nobody acts.
drag 103 1435 450
wait 1
expect floating 103
expect count docked 0
# The keys work at every setting.
focus 103
press dock left
expect docked 103
# A docked window carried to the left edge is macOS's like any other: out of the arrangement, and Arbor sets no frame on it.
drag 103 0 450
wait 1
expect floating 103
expect size 103 720x875
expect count docked 0
press dock left
expect docked 103
# Let go a few points short of an edge, where macOS never acts and Arbor offers nothing, it goes home: at the left, the right and the top alike.
drag 103 1435 450
expect docked 103
expect moved-once
drag 103 900 30
expect docked 103
expect moved-once
# The bottom edge is Arbor's at every setting, and so are the edges of a docked window.
drag 101 900 897
expect docked 101
# With the switches off, every edge is Arbor's.
mac-tiling off
drag 102 1437 450
expect docked 102
